Subject: Technical Inquiry Regarding FSM Controller Specifications



Dear AOS Team,

I hope this email finds you well.

Following the quotation we recently received from your team, I am writing t=
o you as the Head of the Control Department at ARC. We are currently review=
ing the proposal to integrate your Fast Steering Mirror (FSM) systems into =
our upcoming project, and before finalizing our decision, we would like to =
clarify a few technical aspects regarding the controller.

Specifically, we would appreciate it if you could provide more information,=
 documentation, or datasheets covering the following points:

  *   Communication Interfaces: Aside from the digital Ethernet interface m=
entioned in your documentation, what other communication protocols, industr=
ial buses, or APIs are supported by the controller?

     *   The FSM controller can also receive position commands using SPI an=
d RS485 interfaces.  The Ethernet communication interface is intended to be=
 a supervisory and configuration interface and is not typically used as the=
 real-time command interface.

  *   Bandwidth and Tuning: How flexible is the adjustable bandwidth via th=
e digital Ethernet interface, and what specific tools or software are provi=
ded for real-time loop tuning and performance monitoring?

     *   The FSM Controller GUI provides full control of the internal contr=
ol settings with configurable parameters, allowing for complete bandwidth c=
ontrol within the total performance envelope of the FSM, as well as flexibl=
e filtering at multiple points in the control chain:

        *   Proportional, Integral, and Derivative gain coefficients for X =
and Y axis
        *   Position sensor input 2nd-order biquad filter coefficients
        *   Derivative branch 2nd-order biquad filter coefficients
        *   Two cascaded controller output filter 2nd-order biquad coeffici=
ents

     *   The FSM Controller GUI can command the FSM to execute the followin=
g characterization tests and retrieve 4-second long full-rate 500kHz logs f=
or out-of-band analysis:

        *   Standard - No additional position changes are enacted, useful f=
or viewing the performance of the FSM with the position caged or uncaged.  =
The controller can be configured to inject a frequency or white noise distu=
rbance during this characterization test as well.
        *   Step Response - Configurable Start and End positions in both X =
and Y axes, step command is executed at approximately 400ms into the 4s log
        *   Frequency Sweep - Using Direct Digital Synthesis built-in to th=
e FSM electronics, one can specify a start and end frequency and the contro=
ller will inject either position commands or a disturbance with the frequen=
cy sweep.
        *   Open-loop command pulse - Allows for an at-rest FSM to be "shov=
ed" by an open loop command pulse to derive impulse response in-system

     *   The FSM Controller GUI includes a self-tune procedure which execut=
es full-rate-logged step responses and analyzes the rise-time, settle-time,=
 and overshoot to determine a performance metric and then automatically per=
forms stochastic axial search to optimize P, I, and D gains for X and Y con=
troller parameters.  We have found this automated process greatly reduces m=
anual labor time tuning a FSM in-situ.

  *   Software Integration & Examples: Do you provide sample code, software=
 development kits (SDKs), or integration examples (such as Python, C++, or =
LabVIEW scripts) to interface with the controller?

     *   We have an example FSM Controller GUI developed in Visual Studio i=
n C# that implements the Ethernet connection and provides access to the dat=
a structures that drive all of the configuration parameters.

  *   Hardware & I/O Specifications: What are the exact power supply requir=
ements (voltage/current) for the controller unit, and what type of physical=
 connectors are used for inputs, outputs, and sensor feedback?

     *   The bare FSM controller electronics requires +28VDC with a support=
ed transient current (for large, fast steps) of 10A. Quiescent current with=
 the FSM caged and stationary tends to hover around 400mA.
     *   Standard interfaces are listed below:

        *   +28VDC Power: 4-pin Mate-N-LOK connector or supplied via backpl=
ane and converted from 120VAC in 3U chassis configuration
        *   Sensor and Drive: 15-pin DSUB
        *   RS485: 9-pin DSUB
        *   SPI: Backplane

  *   Latency and Determinism: What is the internal processing latency of t=
he digital controller, and does it support real-time deterministic operatio=
n for high-speed jitter rejection?

     *   All digital control is implemented in the FGPA and is completely d=
eterministic.  The tightly-coupled ARM processor and Ethernet are strictly =
supervisory/configuration interfaces

To ensure we have a complete understanding of how the system will integrate=
 into our existing control architecture, would it be possible to schedule a=
 brief technical meeting with one of your engineers next week to discuss th=
ese details?

Thank you for your continued support and assistance. I look forward to hear=
ing from you.

Best regards,







